WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.160
Security work starts with mapping the MCP environment.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.840
The diagram should show the AI host application, MCP client, MCP servers, tools,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.480
resources, prompt templates, user identity, service identity, downstream APIs,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.380
local files, cloud services, secrets, and tokens.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.560
It should distinguish local servers from remote servers because local execution and remote

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.850
service access create different risk profiles.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.300
A local server may touch files or processes on the user's machine,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.760
while a remote server may rely on network trust, tenant isolation,

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.250
and external service controls.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.480
A useful architecture diagram shows data flow, authority flow, and action flow.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.905
Data flow answers what information enters the model context, tool arguments, logs,

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:41.165
resources, and downstream services.

00:00:41.165 --> 00:00:44.520
Authority flow answers which identity or token is used when a tool acts.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.635
Action flow answers what can be read, written, sent, created, deleted, approved,

00:00:48.635 --> 00:00:49.240
or changed.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.889
These flows do not always line up.

00:00:50.889 --> 00:00:53.880
A user may ask a question, the model may propose an action,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.580
and a tool may execute under a service account with broader permissions than the user

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:58.120
expected.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.960
Trust boundaries appear between user input, model output, tool metadata, tool execution,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.240
external content, and downstream systems.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.380
User input is not automatically safe.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.740
Model output is not automatically authorized.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:14.075
Tool descriptions are not neutral; they influence how the model thinks about tool use.

00:01:14.075 --> 00:01:17.800
External resources may contain stale, misleading, or hostile instructions.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.200
Downstream systems may enforce their own policies,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.000
but the MCP application still decides what to request and what context to expose.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.680
Architecture visibility prevents vague risk discussions.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.540
Instead of saying an agent can use tools,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.360
the team can say which host connects to which servers, which tools are available,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.120
which resources are exposed, which credentials are used, and where approvals happen.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.160
That clarity supports least privilege, monitoring, incident response, and governance.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.220
Without it, teams end up trusting the model, the tool, the server,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.415
and the user interface as one blended boundary,

00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:49.600
which is exactly what MCP security needs to avoid.
